-- the tautomers of a molecule as one type: its proton-transfer forms as points, each admissible
-- transfer as a path between them. A function out of this type must respect the transfers
-- (taut-rec), so a quantity that differs between tautomeric forms cannot be stated of the
-- substance at all — which is exactly what InChI's partial tautomer invariance leaves unstated
-- (IUPAC #2012-023-2-800, open since 2012), and what the field works around by choosing one form.
data Taut (A : Type 0) (step : A → A → Bool) = taut-pt (a : A) | taut-tr (a : A) (b : A) (p : isTrue (step a b)) (i : I) [ i = 0 ↦ taut-pt a , i = 1 ↦ taut-pt b ]
